# Enclave resolver — the egress BACKSTOP for the enclave tier.
#
# Rendered by deploy.sh from the deployment's addressing; do not edit the generated Corefile.
#
# It is NOT the resolver services are given, and cannot be: podman overrides a container's
# `dns:` whenever its own resolver is enabled on the network, and disabling that resolver is
# exactly what would force every service back to a pinned address. Dynamic names and a resolver
# we control cannot both be had on this runtime.
#
# What podman does with `dns:` is make this the runtime resolver's UPSTREAM. While the network
# is internal there is nothing to forward — in-zone names are answered directly and outside
# names die there — so this file sees no traffic and the egress control is the network itself.
#
# It arms the moment a network gains a route out. Then every name the runtime cannot answer is
# forwarded here and refused. That is not hypothetical: it is how the DMZ-link leak was caught,
# with the enclave's own bridge container resolving live internet addresses because that one
# network had been left non-internal.
#
# THE POLICY, in one sentence: enclave services resolve each other and exactly one DMZ name,
# and every other query is REFUSED.
#
# Why REFUSED and not a silent failure: the enclave network is `internal: true`, so there is
# already no upstream and an external lookup dies as NXDOMAIN. That is a property of the
# network, not a decision anyone wrote down, and it disappears the moment someone attaches a
# gateway to debug something. An explicit REFUSED states the intent, survives that change, and
# is visible in a query log as a denial rather than as an absence.
#
# Addresses stay DYNAMIC. In-zone lookups are forwarded to the container runtime's own
# resolver, which knows where each service currently is, so nothing here pins a service to an
# address. The only pinned address in this design is this resolver's own, because resolv.conf
# can hold nothing but a literal.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The enclave's own services: backend, worker, db, redis, minio, keycloak,
# traefik, oauth2-proxy, consul, frontend, puller.
#
# Forwarded to the runtime resolver on the enclave network. It answers only for containers
# attached to that network, so this block cannot reach the DMZ or anything beyond it — the
# scope of the answer set is the network itself, not a list maintained here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__RUNTIME_ZONE__:53 {
    errors
    # Denials and answers are both worth seeing: a service that starts asking for names it
    # should not need is the first sign of something wrong inside the enclave.
    log
    forward . __ENCLAVE_DNS__ {
        # No fallback. If the runtime resolver cannot answer, the answer is "no" — never a
        # second opinion from somewhere less trusted.
        policy sequential
        max_fails 0
    }
    cache 30
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single permitted cross-tier name: the DMZ evidence receiver.
#
# The puller dials it outbound — enclave to DMZ, the one direction the architecture allows —
# so this name must resolve or evidence never comes inward. It is a MORE SPECIFIC zone than
# the block above, so it wins for this name only; no other DMZ service is nameable from here.
#
# Nothing in the DMZ can resolve an enclave name in return. That asymmetry is deliberate: a
# compromised DMZ host should not be able to enumerate what it might reach.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
receiver.__RUNTIME_ZONE__:53 {
    errors
    log
    forward . __DMZ_DNS__ {
        policy sequential
        max_fails 0
    }
    cache 30
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else: REFUSED.
#
# No recursion, no forwarders, no root hints. A name outside the two zones above has no
# answer and no path to one, which closes DNS as an exfiltration channel from the tier that
# holds the evidence — the worker parses hazardous memory images, and a resolver that would
# look anything up for it is a way out.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.:53 {
    errors
    log
    template ANY ANY {
        rcode REFUSED
    }
}
